  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 3366\n\n- Rain event Saturday with placement still somewhat uncertain\n\nAfter a gloomy and drizzly day for most of the area on Thursday, \ntoday is shaping up to be a better day before rain chances increase \nonce again tonight. \n\nThe front that has been just south of the forecast area over the \nlast couple of days has moved further south, taking its moisture \nwith it. This is the result of a stronger push of drier air coming \nin from the NE with strengthening high pressure over Canada. This \nwill be short lived however.\n\nA long wave upper low dives over the Western U.S. by Saturday, and \nhelps to establish an upper flow from the WSW. We will see upper \nshort waves eject, and help to lift the warm front back northward \ntoward the area. The main feature is a 35 knot low level jet ahead \nof the wave and intersecting the front to bring an area of fairly \nwidespread rain.\n\nEven though we are approaching this event, the details are far from \ncertain yet. This is because we really have multiple forcing \nmechanisms trying to affect the area, and interacting with each \nother. The low level jet will be transporting precipitable water \nvalues of 2+ inches over the area through Saturday morning. Just as \nthe low level jet bis relaxing and starting to move out, we see some \nupper level jet dynamics in the form of RRQ dynamics lead to some \nadditional mid level fgen working on the moist air mass. There is \nalso a trough coming through at 700 mb leading to more upward \nforcing.\n\nSome models are placing the heavy rain over the I-96 corridor, while \nothers are placing it over the far SW corner of the forecast area. \nIt is tough to pick one or the other, but the larger scale synoptic \nmodels are likely to handle this better than the mesoscale models \ngiven the multiple forcing features.\n\nThe good thing is that the impacts will be limited from this event. \nThunder will be possible, but will be elevated, so severe weather is \nnot likely. Rain could be locally heavy, but it not expected to be \ntoo heavy over a widespread area, so no headlines are needed at this \ntime. \n\n- Diminishing rain chances Saturday night and Sunday\n\nThe multiple forcing features, and how they interact with each other \ncreate the main uncertainty with the forecast. This includes some of \nthe model solutions/ensemble members holding some rain over Southern \nLower into Sunday. The departing low level jet and upper trough \npushing through should tend to push the moisture and rainfall out a \nbit more given the scenario. \n\n- Cooler pattern much of next week with chances of rain showers\n\nThe upper trough comes in over the area as the main long wave trough \nover the west is pushed toward the area. This upper trough \nencounters additional energy and has been shown for multiple runs to \nclose off an upper low over the region. If this indeed happens, we \nwill be looking at a cool, and unsettled weather pattern much of \nnext week. Unfortunately, the development and then subsequent \nmovement of the complex is one of the most uncertain things to try \nand forecast. The good thing is it can be summarized well with \ncooler temps and a chance of rain each day, but it will not rain all \nof the time.",
